  "query_preview": "..\nGOES sounder profile and SPC mesoanalysis shows the atmosphere \neroding the last remaining CIN values across the area. There \nhas been ample sunshine to continue eroding CIN and there are a\nfew towering cumulus clouds forming over the eastern Sky \nIslands. All indications for an active afternoon for areas south\nand east of Tucson. These areas have the best ingredients to \nsupport thunderstorms due to shear values of 10-20 kts and CAPE \ngreater than 1500 j/kg. A few storms will reach Tucson metro to \nbring gusty winds and light to moderate rain showers. 12Z HREF \nmodel has 70% chances for wind gusts greater than 35 mph in \nCochise County and into eastern Santa Cruz County this \nafternoon. There can be a few storms capable producing a severe \nlevel wind in the afternoon. Storm's will be capable producing \nup to 0.75-1 inch total with Santa Cruz County having at least \n50% of receiving an inch or more through the late evening. \nThere will be a risk for flash flooding along the International \nBorder between Nogales and Naco. \n\nThe ridge of high pressure will start to build west starting\ntomorrow. Temperatures will be increasing 10-15 degrees above \nseasonal normals. This will push the region into Major and \nExtreme HeatRisk tomorrow through Sunday. The hottest days will \nbe Friday and Saturday with temperatures reaching up to 113F. \nTucson has 112F on Saturday. Extreme Heat Warning is in effect \nThursday through Sunday evening. \n\nThunderstorm chances will be generally low tomorrow and Friday\ndue the lack of upper level support. There will be plenty of\ninstability to get vertical growth. The best areas for any \nthunderstorm growth will be south and east of Tucson. Chances \nwill increase for Saturday and Sunday.\n\nNext week, right after this heat, the upper level flow will \nreturn from the east to southeast with the high pressure center \nclose to the Four Corners. This set up will provide better daily\nchances for thunderstorms and rain showers.",
